A desk study that prepares an industrial company's investment decision on a CO2-reducing pilot, demonstration or proven-technology project in the Netherlands, through measures other than hydrogen, green chemistry or gasification, such as electrification.
STUDI (Studies voor Duurzame Industrie, studies for sustainable industry, formerly TSE Industrie studies) funds three types of study:
- a feasibility study (haalbaarheidsstudie) on a pilot project, in which an experimental prototype is tested in a realistic setting;
- an environmental study (milieustudie) on a demonstration project or a project with proven technology, about investments that raise environmental protection;
- a comparable study (vergelijkbare studie) on a demonstration or proven-technology project, funded as de-minimis aid (small amounts of state aid outside the usual EU rules).
The follow-up project must help reduce CO2 emissions in Dutch industry cost-effectively within ten years after the study ends. This call is programme line 1 (CO2-reducing measures in industry other than hydrogen, green chemistry and gasification of residual streams).
Programme line 1 covers every CO2-reducing measure in industry except hydrogen and green chemistry (programme line 2) and gasification of residual streams (programme line 3), for example studies on:
- making a process more energy-efficient;
- replacing primary raw materials with recycled or recovered ones;
- capturing CO2 for use in greenhouses, synthetic fuels or long-term storage;
- replacing machines and systems that use fossil fuels such as natural gas with electric ones (electrification).

Mainly desk research that answers the questions needed for an investment decision; research and development itself is excluded.
In scope:
- literature research, market exploration and competitor analysis;
- permit research and legal feasibility research;
- working out the business case;
- pre-engineering and parts of FEED (front-end engineering design, the detailed technical design before construction);
- limited test work, such as testing samples, when needed to answer the feasibility questions, including preparing a test plan or buying test material.
Excluded:
- industrial research and experimental development, and test work aimed at improving or developing a product, process or service;
- studies whose costs consist for more than half of test work;
- parts of the study not needed for the investment decision;
- studies on projects that invest in machines or production installations using fossil fuels, including natural gas, unless they only add environmental protection without raising capacity or fossil fuel use;
- studies on underground storage of CO2 on land;
- studies on CO2 capture at fossil-fuelled electricity or heat production, except waste incineration plants;
- studies for a follow-up project that already received a subsidy for its implementation, unless that subsidy was the SDE++.
